  "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $3-2=1.$",
